# origin-http — notes

Baseline Phase 1 service adapter: one bounded fetch of an allowlisted origin.

## Behaviour

- `prepare()` is pure: origin-form target, `Host`, `User-Agent` (device's, else
  the gateway's), `Accept-Encoding: identity`, `Connection: close`.
- `execute()` is the only I/O point:
  - connects to the **IP that passed egress policy** (pinned) while TLS SNI and
    certificate verification use the requested hostname;
  - upstream TLS is the Python default context (current TLS, current trust
    store) — never weakened to suit a device;
  - response headers are size-capped and allowlisted before entering the IR;
  - response body is read up to `max_response_bytes` (truncation is flagged);
  - `gzip`/`deflate` responses are decoded within the same bound; any other
    `Content-Encoding` is a failure, not a passthrough;
  - redirects are **never followed** — the 3xx is handed to the device with a
    gateway URL, so egress policy is re-checked on every hop.
- `allowed_schemes` defaults to `["https"]`; `http` is opt-in via config.

## Policy

`policy.json` mirrors `DEFAULT_EGRESS` in `adapter.py` (a test keeps them in
sync). Host allowlist and private-range blocking are deployment config — this
adapter is not an open proxy.

## Status

Level 2+ ("Probed"/"Functional" against local fakes) — see `docs/DEVICE_ADAPTERS.md`
§5 for the support ladder. No live-Internet claims are made for this adapter.
